"""Fixtures for the suite.

{% if has_api %}The important one is ``client``, and what makes it interesting is the database.{% else %}The important one is ``rolled_back``, and what makes it interesting is the
database.{% endif %}

**Tests run against a real Postgres, inside a transaction that is rolled back.**
There is no fake database and no SQLite substitute, on purpose: the queries are
the part most likely to be wrong, and a double that does not run them is not
testing anything. What makes that fast enough to run on every save is the undo —
``keel.testing.rolled_back_database`` opens one connection, begins a transaction,
binds a database whose sessions join it with ``join_transaction_mode=
"create_savepoint"``, and rolls back at the end of the test. Application code
calls ``uow()`` and commits normally; the commit releases a savepoint and the
outer transaction still owns the undo.

Two consequences worth knowing before a test surprises you:

* Every row created inside one test shares a ``created_at``. Postgres' ``now()``
  is the *transaction* timestamp, and the whole test is one transaction — so
  assert on sets rather than on timestamp ordering.
* The cache is switched to the in-process ``array`` driver, so the suite needs
  Postgres but not Redis. ``keel.testing.fake_cache`` is the tool when a test
  wants to assert on what was cached.
{% if has_worker %}
**The queue is switched to ``null``, and that is not the same choice.** The cache
gets a real in-process driver because a test asking "was this cached?" wants
real TTLs and real serialisation. A queue gets ``null`` because running the job
would make a dispatch test exercise the *handler* — its writes, its outbound
calls, its failure modes — while claiming to test the code that dispatched it.
"Did this enqueue the job?" and "does the job work?" are two tests, and
``keel.testing.fake_queue`` and a direct ``await job.handle()`` are how each is
written. See ``tests/test_jobs.py``.
{% endif %}
{% if has_api and has_worker %}**Two runtimes, one set of subsystems.** ``client`` drives the real application
through its lifespan; ``rolled_back`` binds the same subsystems with no HTTP at
all, which is the environment a worker process runs in. Both undo themselves the
same way.
{% endif %}"""

